Do housing markets efficiently price the physical depreciation of public ca=
pital? We investigate this question using a quasi-experimental design cente=
red on narrow-margin referendums to renew local road maintenance property t=
axes. By combining decades of housing
 transactions with a novel ConvNeXt V2 computer vision model fine-tuned to =
measure physical road decay from satellite imagery, we provide evidence of =
the capitalization of infrastructure disinvestment. While tax cuts provide =
an immediate 11% reduction in maintenance
 funding, house prices only drop in the fourth year, the same year road det=
erioration is statistically observed. Physical road quality declines by 23%=
 over ten years, and house prices drop by 9% ($15,350 on average). The resu=
lting loss in household wealth dwarfs
 the annual tax savings by a factor of 200, suggesting a massive welfare in=
efficiency driven by fiscal illusion. While standard asset-pricing models u=
nder rational expectations predict an immediate capitalization of future se=
rvice-flow reductions, we find that
 house prices only reflect disinvestment once physical deterioration become=
s observable. Our results show how coordination failure can lead to substan=
tial net losses in household equity through the slow depreciation of the pu=
blic commons.</div>
<div class=3D"x_x_x_x_x_x_elementToProof" style=3D"margin:0in; font-family:=
