fficiently internalize the long-term consequences of infrastructure disinve=
stment.&nbsp; While a vast literature has established the positive capitali=
zation of new public investment (Oates,
 1969; Cellini, et al., 2010), we know surprisingly little about how and wh=
en markets price the physical depreciation of the existing public commons.&=
nbsp; In theory, rational expectations should trigger an immediate adjustme=
nt in property values the moment a fiscal
 decision to reduce maintenance funding is made.&nbsp; In practice, if info=
rmation frictions or &quot;fiscal illusions&quot; persist, the economic cos=
t of disinvestment may remain hidden until physical decay becomes tangible.=
&nbsp; We investigate this disconnect by leveraging
